 ## Background
-Free-path sampling is a fundamental technique in volumetric path tracing, originating from the need to simulate light transport in participating media. It is based on the physical principle that the distance to the next interaction in a homogeneous medium follows an exponential distribution determined by the extinction coefficient. The method is described in [S1, unofficial] as a way to avoid distance and spherical integrations by letting rays randomly bounce in the medium. It was noted that while unbiased, it could produce noticeable noise, leading some projects to abandon it for more stable methods like equi-angular sampling for specific cases [S1, unofficial].
+Free-path sampling is a fundamental technique in volumetric path tracing, originating from the need to simulate light transport in participating media [S1, unofficial]. It is based on the physical principle that the distance to the next interaction in a homogeneous medium follows an exponential distribution determined by the extinction coefficient [G]. The method is described as a way to avoid distance and spherical integrations by letting the light ray randomly bounce off in the middle of participating media before hitting a surface [S1, unofficial]. It was noted that while unbiased, it could produce noticeable noise, leading some implementations to abandon it for more stable methods like equi-angular sampling for specific cases [S1, unofficial].
 
